items.append({
"identity":"note-fidelity/not-same-read/missing/aspects.name-uniqueness/ActiveRecord::FinderMethods#exists?/MISSING",
"source":"agent","catchable":True,
"evidence":("Real statement, captured in "+REC+", scenario share-with-new-local-contact (and again in inflected-locale-reads-profile), under frame ActiveRecord::FinderMethods#exists?, which in that scenario's target_calls sits nested inside ActiveRecord::Base#save: "
"`SELECT  1 AS one FROM \"aspects\" WHERE LOWER(\"aspects\".\"name\") = LOWER(?) AND \"aspects\".\"id\" != ? AND \"aspects\".\"user_id\" = ? LIMIT ?` (name 'Aspect Exists'). "
"The corpus (417 dumps) carries exactly three distinct notes under ActiveRecord::FinderMethods#exists?, none on `aspects`: the blocks existence check, and two contacts existence checks. No target anywhere in the corpus carries a note that reads `aspects` by `name`. Mechanical verdict: MISSING. "
"READING OF SOURCE (an argument, not a measurement): app/models/aspect.rb:19 `validates_uniqueness_of :name, :scope => :user_id, :case_sensitive => false`. On the new-contact arm, `contact.aspects << aspect` (app/models/user/connecting.rb:21) adds the aspect to an UNSAVED contact's has_many :through, so `contact.save` (connecting.rb:22) validates every target of that association, the aspect included, and the uniqueness validator issues this read. The real save on that arm also re-runs the Contact validations (a second `Contact Exists`, `User Load`, `Block Exists`). The corpus records NONE of the nested reads. In the dumps (e.g. dump_auth_json_dse0007.json) `ActiveRecord::Base#save` at connecting.rb:22 is a single mocked call, and it notes only `INSERT INTO \"contacts\" ...`. The mock replaces the body that would have run the validations. The re-run Contact reads have the same shapes as reads recorded elsewhere. The aspects-by-name read has no counterpart at all. "
"The policy IR (in/policy.json, 59 atoms) has only two Access shapes on `aspects`: `SELECT \"aspects\".* FROM \"aspects\" WHERE \"aspects\".\"user_id\" = $$(p0) AND \"aspects\".\"id\" = $$(p1) ORDER BY order_id ASC LIMIT 1` (p1 = $SYM_PARAM_aspect_id) and `SELECT \"aspects\".* FROM \"aspects\" WHERE \"aspects\".\"id\" = $$(p0)` (p0 = a membership's aspect_id). Both are scoped to the ONE requested aspect."),
"consequence":("If true, this is NOT the same read: the mock UNDERSTATES what the endpoint touches. MISSING is never declarable as an instrument artefact. On every successful new-contact share, the real request asks whether ANY OTHER aspect of the principal has the same case-folded name (`id != ?`), which reads the names of the principal's other aspects. No view the policy ships admits that, because both `aspects` views are pinned to a single aspect id. An enforcer running the folded policy would refuse a statement the app issues on its main success path. The fix is in the `ActiveRecord::Base#save` mock (it must note or replay the validation reads of autosaved through-records), which is the target layer and not mine to change. "
"RULE P: the existing plan already catches the class, so the acceptance should become a commitment to KEEP the scenario. real_mode_plan.json `scenarios` entry `share-with-new-local-contact` (file local_amc1/plan/real_mode_plan.json; the config's `real_mode_plan` key names it) is the scenario that exhibits this statement, and checks.shape_check over this recording should report it as statement-shape/note-mismatch/ActiveRecord::FinderMethods#exists? by its stated contract. I did not run shape_check. To catch the same class on the PERSISTED-contact save arm, see the proposal item note-fidelity/undecidable/plan-gap/existing-non-receiving-contact. "
"FALSIFIER: if a recording of a new-contact share issued no `aspects` name-uniqueness statement, or if a corpus note under any target carried `LOWER(\"aspects\".\"name\")`, this claim is wrong.")})

items.append({
"identity":"note-fidelity/not-same-read/instrument-load/blocks.user_id/ActiveRecord::Querying#find_by_sql/STAR-OVER",
"source":"agent","catchable":False,
"evidence":("Corpus note, verbatim, from dump_auth_json_dse0001.json, target ActiveRecord::Querying#find_by_sql at statement_cache.rb:108, refs p0=SYM_RESULT_ActiveRecord__FinderMethods_devise_user_first_1_id: "
"`SELECT \"blocks\".* FROM \"blocks\" WHERE \"blocks\".\"user_id\" = $$(p0)`. It appears 754 times in the 417 dumps, once before every blocks `exists?` note. The policy IR ships it as Access atoms (`SELECT \"blocks\".* FROM \"blocks\" WHERE \"blocks\".\"user_id\" = $$(p0)` with p0 = c1.id, c7.user_id, c9.user_id), so the folded policy grants the principal's ENTIRE block list. "
"Real side, from "+REC+": across all 9 scenarios, 83 statements, there is NO `SELECT \"blocks\".*` statement. Every blocks access is `SELECT  1 AS one FROM \"blocks\" WHERE \"blocks\".\"user_id\" = ? AND \"blocks\".\"person_id\" = ? LIMIT ?` under ActiveRecord::FinderMethods#exists?. That includes the blocked-person scenario, whose 4 statements end at that exists?. In each scenario's target_calls, no ActiveRecord::Querying#find_by_sql frame sits next to the blocks exists?. "
"READING OF SOURCE (an argument; I did not boot the runtime): app code `blocks.where(person_id: person.id).exists?` (connecting.rb:14) never loads the association. The load comes from the instrument. targets/endpoints/aspect_memberships_create.rb:319-320 declares `User#blocks` as a SHIM returning `receiver.association(:blocks).reader`, an AR CollectionProxy. call_interceptor.rb at the packet's runtime_revision ee9f17b, lines 704-707, then evaluates `concrete_result == true || concrete_result == false` on that return value. In activerecord-5.2.4.3, collection_proxy.rb:959-961 defines `def ==(other) load_target == other end`, so that comparison loads the association: CollectionAssociation#find_target, then StatementCache#execute (statement_cache.rb:108), then find_by_sql. That is the frame and line the dumps record. The target layer's own comment (§10, ~line 938: 'the association genuinely LOADS') assumes the load is the app's. The recording contradicts that. Mechanical verdict as the ladder would name it: STAR-OVER, with no real statement of this shape to compare against."),
"consequence":("If true, this is NOT the same read. The corpus OVERSTATES the endpoint: it records a whole-collection read of the principal's blocks that no real request issues, and it is caused by the runtime comparing a shim's AR proxy with `==`, not by the app. The shipped policy then grants `blocks WHERE user_id = <principal>` when the endpoint needs only an existence test on (user_id, person_id). This is an over-grant of the principal's own rows, not a cross-user leak. It is wrong as a statement of what the endpoint touches, and on any endpoint whose SHIM returns an AR relation or proxy it would invent reads the same way. "
"catchable=false, JUSTIFIED: every mechanical check this domain has runs in one direction, real statement to corpus note. checks.shape_check asks whether each REAL statement has a note. statement_differential asks whether the folded policy names the relation of each real statement. A NOTE with no real counterpart is invisible to both, and no scenario, fixture row or must_stay_symbolic input makes either one see it. The blocked-person scenario already exercises the arm and the over-read still went unflagged. Catching the class needs a new check (note-to-real: each corpus note shape on a path some scenario covers must be matched by a real statement) or a runtime fix (the interceptor must not call `==` on an opaque shim return). Both are cross-endpoint. Escalated separately as ctl/ask/interceptor-shim-proxy-load.json (class shared). "
"FALSIFIER: if a real request on this endpoint issues `SELECT \"blocks\".* FROM \"blocks\" WHERE \"blocks\".\"user_id\" = ?`, or if replacing `concrete_result == true || concrete_result == false` with an identity test (`equal?`) leaves the 754 find_by_sql notes in the next corpus, this claim is wrong.")})

items.append({
"identity":"note-fidelity/not-same-read/new-record-no-pk/aspect_memberships.contact_id/ActiveRecord::FinderMethods#first/PRED-OP-DIFF",
"source":"agent","catchable":False,
"evidence":("Corpus, new-contact arm (12 dumps; verbatim from dump_auth_json_dse0007.json, where `find_by_1_not_found` is taken): "
"(a) ActiveRecord::Base#save at connecting.rb:22 notes `INSERT INTO \"contacts\" (\"user_id\", \"person_id\", \"created_at\", \"updated_at\", \"receiving\") VALUES ($$(p0), $$(p1), '<concolic_now>', '<concolic_now>', true)`. "
"(b) ActiveRecord::Base#save at core.rb:873 notes `INSERT INTO \"aspect_memberships\" (\"aspect_id\", \"created_at\", \"updated_at\") VALUES ($$(p0), '<concolic_now>', '<concolic_now>')`, with NO contact_id column. "
"(c) ActiveRecord::FinderMethods#first at aspect_memberships_controller.rb:51 notes `SELECT \"aspect_memberships\".* FROM \"aspect_memberships\" WHERE \"aspect_memberships\".\"contact_id\" IS NULL AND \"aspect_memberships\".\"aspect_id\" = $$(p0) ORDER BY \"aspect_memberships\".\"id\" ASC LIMIT 1`. "
"Both (b) and (c) are in the policy IR as Access atoms (refs p0=c3.id / c5.id). "
"Real side, MEASURED in "+REC+", scenario share-with-new-local-contact, under ActiveRecord::Base#save: `INSERT INTO \"aspect_memberships\" (\"aspect_id\", \"contact_id\", \"created_at\", \"updated_at\") VALUES (?, ?, ?, ?)`. The real write carries contact_id, so the mock's write (b) differs measurably. "
"The real counterpart of (c) is NOT in the recording. That scenario (and inflected-locale-reads-profile) ends with `Redis::CannotConnectError` on the Sidekiq push after COMMIT, before controller line 51. On the PERSISTED arm (scenario share-existing-receiving-contact) the real read is `SELECT  \"aspect_memberships\".* FROM \"aspect_memberships\" WHERE \"aspect_memberships\".\"contact_id\" = ? AND \"aspect_memberships\".\"aspect_id\" = ? ORDER BY \"aspect_memberships\".\"id\" ASC LIMIT ?`. "
"READING OF SOURCE for the new arm: the controller (aspect_memberships_controller.rb:51-52) reads `AspectMembership.where(contact_id: @contact.id, aspect_id: @aspect.id).first` AFTER `share_with` has saved the contact, and Rails assigns `id` from the INSERT. So the real statement on this arm has `\"contact_id\" = ?` bound to the new contact's id. The mock's `IS NULL` shows that the `ActiveRecord::Base#save` mock gives a NEW record no primary key, symbolic or otherwise. Every later use of `contact.id` then sees nil: the autosaved membership INSERT drops contact_id and the controller's read becomes `IS NULL`. Mechanical verdict for (c) against its real counterpart: PRED-OP-DIFF (`IS NULL` vs `=`)."),
"consequence":("If true, this is NOT the same read, and the error runs both ways on the new-contact arm. The policy admits a read of membership rows with NULL contact_id in the requested aspect, which the app never issues. No atom on that arm names the read the app DOES issue: the new contact's membership, keyed on an id the corpus never minted. The write spec for the join row also lacks contact_id, which is the column that ties the membership to the contact. The cause is the save mock's model of a new record (no PK assigned), not preload arity or any other runtime limit. It is a mock defect in the target layer, which I may not edit. "
"catchable=false, JUSTIFIED: no config change makes a current check see this. shape_check is path-insensitive. The real `\"contact_id\" = ?` read, and the real 4-column INSERT, both match notes recorded on the PERSISTED arm (`\"contact_id\" = $$(p0)` and the 4-column INSERT under save/save!), so a recording of the new arm, even if the Sidekiq push succeeded, would pass. The over-read (`IS NULL`) has no real counterpart, so the one-directional checks cannot see it. Catching the class needs path-aware comparison (the note on THIS arm against the real statement on THIS arm) or a save mock that mints a symbolic PK. "
"Related, and it is config: the real-mode rig cannot record ANY post-commit statement on the new-contact arm while the Sidekiq push raises. A new contact always has receiving=false, so needs_dispatch is always true there. Making Redis reachable in the realmode box, or running the probe with Sidekiq in testing/fake mode, would put the real line-51 read into the recording. I can't tell which of those the probe supports, so I'm stating the need, not a key. "
"FALSIFIER: if a real new-contact share issues the membership read with `\"contact_id\" IS NULL`, or if the corpus's save mock is shown to assign an id that a later note carries, this claim is wrong.")})

