items.append({
"identity":"note-fidelity/same-read/persisted-id-model/contacts.id/ActiveRecord::FinderMethods#exists?/PRED-OP-DIFF",
"source":"agent","catchable":True,
"evidence":("Real statement, "+REC+", scenario share-existing-receiving-contact, under ActiveRecord::FinderMethods#exists? (twice: once in contact.valid?, once nested in save): `SELECT  1 AS one FROM \"contacts\" WHERE \"contacts\".\"person_id\" = ? AND \"contacts\".\"id\" != ? AND \"contacts\".\"user_id\" = ? LIMIT ?`. "
"Corpus note, verbatim (dump_auth_json_dse0001.json, exists? at core.rb:2735, p0=..._find_by_1_person_id, p1=..._find_by_1_user_id; 340 occurrences): `SELECT 1 AS one FROM \"contacts\" WHERE \"contacts\".\"person_id\" = $$(p0) AND \"contacts\".\"id\" IS NOT NULL AND \"contacts\".\"user_id\" = $$(p1) LIMIT 1`. Mechanical verdict: PRED-OP-DIFF (`!= ?` vs `IS NOT NULL`). "
"READING OF SOURCE: activerecord-5.2.4.3 validations/uniqueness.rb:24-27 adds `where.not(primary_key => record.id_in_database)` for a persisted record. `IS NOT NULL` is exactly `where.not(id: nil)`, so in the corpus the found contact is persisted? but has `id_in_database == nil`. The same row's id IS symbolic elsewhere: the save note `UPDATE \"contacts\" ... WHERE \"contacts\".\"id\" = $$(p0)` binds p0=SYM_RESULT_ActiveRecord__FinderMethods_find_by_1_id. I conclude the mock sets the symbolic id as a CURRENT attribute value but not as the value read from the database. This is not a measurement: I did not rerun the corpus."),
"consequence":("If true, the two statements denote the SAME read and the difference comes from how the runtime models a found row, not from what the endpoint touches. Same table, same scoping columns (person_id, user_id) bound to the same contact's columns. The note's predicate is weaker than the real one (it also admits the contact itself). Given the unique index index_contacts_on_user_id_and_person_id (db/schema.rb:137), the note can reveal at most that one row, and the find_by atom `SELECT \"contacts\".* FROM \"contacts\" WHERE \"contacts\".\"user_id\" = $$(p0) AND \"contacts\".\"person_id\" = $$(p1) LIMIT 1` already admits it. So the policy is correct in tables, predicates and ownership here, and the difference changes nothing it grants. If FALSE (if `IS NOT NULL` reflected a real nil id), the persisted-contact arm would be mis-modelled throughout. RULE P: the class is caught mechanically once the recording carries `id != ?` and the shape ladder treats a weaker note predicate as covering it. Scenario share-existing-receiving-contact already supplies that recording, so keep it. "
"FALSIFIER: make the find_by mock's row report its symbolic id from `id_in_database` (i.e. load it as database state, not assign it). If the next corpus still notes `\"contacts\".\"id\" IS NOT NULL`, my mechanism is wrong. If it notes `\"contacts\".\"id\" != $$(p)`, the claim stands.")})

items.append({
"identity":"note-fidelity/same-read/note-omits-limit/single-row-key-reads/LIMIT-DIFF",
"source":"agent","catchable":True,
"evidence":("Real statements in "+REC+", all under ActiveRecord::Querying#find_by_sql (the innermost target frame; the outer frame from target_calls is shown in brackets), each against a corpus note on the outer target that has the SAME table, projection and predicate but NO LIMIT. Mechanical verdict LIMIT-DIFF in the direction OPPOSITE to the people_stream ledger: here the NOTE is the one without the LIMIT. "
"(1) [Core::ClassMethods#find] real `SELECT  \"people\".* FROM \"people\" WHERE \"people\".\"id\" = ? LIMIT ?`; note `SELECT \"people\".* FROM \"people\" WHERE \"people\".\"id\" = $$(p0)` (414). "
"(2) [SingularAssociation#find_target] the same real people statement; note `SELECT \"people\".* FROM \"people\" WHERE \"people\".\"id\" = $$(p0)` (410). "
"(3) [find_target] real `SELECT  \"users\".* FROM \"users\" WHERE \"users\".\"id\" = ? LIMIT ?`; note `SELECT \"users\".* FROM \"users\" WHERE \"users\".\"id\" = $$(p0)` (750). "
"(4) [find_target] real (scenario inflected-locale-reads-profile) `SELECT  \"people\".* FROM \"people\" WHERE \"people\".\"owner_id\" = ? LIMIT ?`; note `SELECT \"people\".* FROM \"people\" WHERE \"people\".\"owner_id\" = $$(p0)` (180). "
"(5) [find_target] real `SELECT  \"profiles\".* FROM \"profiles\" WHERE \"profiles\".\"person_id\" = ? LIMIT ?`; note `SELECT \"profiles\".* FROM \"profiles\" WHERE \"profiles\".\"person_id\" = $$(p0)` (180). "
"(6) [find_target] real (scenario share-existing-receiving-contact) `SELECT  \"aspects\".* FROM \"aspects\" WHERE \"aspects\".\"id\" = ? LIMIT ?`; note `SELECT \"aspects\".* FROM \"aspects\" WHERE \"aspects\".\"id\" = $$(p0)` (42). "
"Rails 5.2 issues `LIMIT 1` for `find`, and for singular-association loads via StatementCache. The mocks render the predicate and drop the limit."),
"consequence":("If true, each pair denotes the SAME read and the difference is the note's rendering, not the endpoint and not preload arity. For (1), (2), (3) and (6) the predicate is equality on a primary key, so LIMIT 1 is redundant. For (4), people.owner_id has a unique index (db/schema.rb:321). For (5), profiles.person_id is NOT unique (schema.rb:462), so the note's view admits every profile row of that person where the request reads one. That is an over-approximation of multiplicity in the note's direction, over the same person's rows, and a view-based policy carries no LIMIT anyway. Net: the policy is correct in tables, predicates and ownership for these six. If FALSE, one of these real statements reads a different row set than its note, and every view folded from these notes is mis-scoped. "
"RULE P: nothing new to add. shape_check compares tables/projection/predicates and ignores LIMIT, so the class is already handled mechanically. I am stating it so that the difference is judged rather than silently passed, not to change anything. "
"FALSIFIER: a real statement on this endpoint that reads one of these relations by the same key WITHOUT the limit and returns more than one row. For (5), a world with two profiles rows for one person whose real request read both.")})

